From 2c2a0d99a06a3d26feaf21beca8998d44d85ec3b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Christopher A. Williamson" <contact@chrisaw.io>
Date: Sun, 26 Apr 2026 16:30:27 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] libprocessgroup: tolerate ENOENT on cgroup.procs write

On vendor kernels that lack the full cgroup v2 hierarchy expected by
APEX services, writing a PID to a non-existent cgroup.procs path fails
with ENOENT. This currently propagates to the caller and kills the
service (e.g. vendor.audio-hal-aidl exits 4 times and triggers init
panic).

Treat ENOENT the same way we already treat ESRCH (thread exiting)
and ENOSPC (empty cpuset): log nothing and return success so the
service continues to boot with degraded cgroup support. This aligns
with the init-level fallback that already makes cgroup creation
failures non-fatal.

Fixes #44
---
 libprocessgroup/task_profiles.cpp | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/libprocessgroup/task_profiles.cpp b/libprocessgroup/task_profiles.cpp
index 2730743..d35322c 100644
--- a/libprocessgroup/task_profiles.cpp
+++ b/libprocessgroup/task_profiles.cpp
@@ -322,6 +322,14 @@ bool SetCgroupAction::AddTidToCgroup(pid_t tid, int fd, ResourceCacheType cache_
         return true;
     }
 
+    // ENOENT is returned when the cgroup path does not exist on vendor
+    // kernels that lack the full hierarchy expected by APEX services.
+    // Treat it as non-fatal so boot can continue with degraded cgroup
+    // support (matching the init-level fallback in service.cpp).
+    if (errno == ENOENT) {
+        return true;
+    }
+
     const char* controller_name = controller()->name();
     // ENOSPC is returned when cpuset cgroup that we are joining has no online cpus
     if (errno == ENOSPC && !strcmp(controller_name, "cpuset")) {
